  "text": "**Review of *The Great Gatsby* by F. Scott Fitzgerald**\n\n*The Great Gatsby* is a timeless novel set in the roaring 1920s, a period of excess, ambition, and social reinvention. The story follows Nick Carraway, a young man from the Midwest who moves to the East to work in the bond business. As he becomes entangled in the lives of his wealthy neighbors, he is drawn into the mysterious world of Jay Gatsby, a self-made millionaire whose lavish parties and enigmatic past intrigue Nick to the point of obsession.\n\nGatsby, whose real name is James Gatz, is a tragic figure whose dream is to win back Daisy Buchanan, his lost love. Daisy, the beautiful and elusive wife of Tom Buchanan, embodies the American Dream and the superficiality of the upper class. Tom, with his wealth and arrogance, is a symbol of the old money elite, and his affair with Myrtle Wilson ultimately leads to tragedy. The Wilsons, working-class and struggling, represent the harsh realities faced by those beneath the glittering surface of the Jazz Age.\n\nThe novel\u2019s setting in West Egg and East Egg symbolizes the divide between new and old money, and the moral decay hidden beneath the opulence. Fitzgerald\u2019s prose is rich with symbolism and metaphors, most notably the green light at the end of Daisy\u2019s dock, which represents Gatsby\u2019s hope and the elusive nature of the American Dream.\n\nThe climax, involving the death of Myrtle Wilson and the subsequent events that lead to Gatsby\u2019s murder, underscores the novel\u2019s themes of idealism vs. reality, and the destructive power of obsession. The tragic ending, with Gatsby\u2019s funeral attended by few, reflects the hollowness of the dreams that the characters pursue.\n\n*The Great Gatsby* masterfully captures the spirit of the Roaring Twenties and offers a poignant critique of the era\u2019s values. Through Nick\u2019s voice, the reader is offered a reflective and often poignant account of a society consumed by wealth, status, and the illusion of happiness. The novel remains a cornerstone of American literature, celebrated for its lyrical writing and enduring relevance.\n\n**Proper Nouns from the Book:**\n- The Great Gatsby\n- F. Scott Fitzgerald\n- The Great Gatsby\n- Jay Gatsby\n- James Gatz\n- Daisy Buchanan\n- Tom Buchanan\n- Myrtle Wilson\n- George Wilson\n- Nick Carraway\n- West Egg\n- East Egg\n- Long Island Sound\n- New York City\n\n**Total Proper Nouns: 12**",
